From: (local patch, not an upstream commit) QM1 Debian Maintainers <qm1-debian@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rtw88: 8723b: stay at 20 MHz until the chip variant
 can be read from the efuse

This is a local policy patch, not an upstream commit.

The upstream tree we build from (05441d697f7f9a927264f4058cbbc6a03dfcac9d,
a torvalds/linux master snapshot of 2026-11-03) reports 40 MHz as a
hardware capability of the RTL8723B:

  efuse->hw_cap.bw = BIT(RTW_CHANNEL_WIDTH_20) | BIT(RTW_CHANNEL_WIDTH_40);

Upstream has since worked out that this is not always true.  The rtw-next
series of 2026-10-20 reads the package type and the chip variant from the
efuse (0x1fb, bits 2:0 and 5:4) and disables 40 MHz when the chip turns out
to be the RTL8703AS, which is a variant of the RTL8723BS that does not
support 40 MHz at all:

  51abe866522f  "wifi: rtw88: 8723b: Read the package type from efuse"
  10d1312f2f19  "wifi: rtw88: 8723b: Read the chip variant from efuse"

We do not carry those two commits.  They need the physical map in the
read_efuse chip operation, which changes the signature of that operation
for every chip in the whole rtw88 tree - a backport across the entire
driver for one bit of information.

So the question is what to do until that information can be read:

  - The chip in the QM1's AzureWave module reports itself as an
    RTL8723BS, but nothing in the tree can tell a genuine RTL8723BS
    from the RTL8703AS variant that upstream found.
  - The community reports for this chip family (the staging rtl8723bs
    driver) show random disconnects with 40 MHz, and the staging
    driver defaults to 40 MHz (rtw_bw_mode=0x01).
  - On the QM1 this Wi-Fi is the only network link: it carries the SSH
    session, the time sync and the provisioning download.  Stability is
    worth more here than the extra throughput of a 40 MHz channel.

Therefore: report 20 MHz only.  The change is one line, and it is the
conservative direction - a link that stays up at 20 MHz beats a link
that drops at 40 MHz.

If the QM1 ever proves that 40 MHz works on this module, the change can
be reverted, or the upstream efuse series can be backported properly.

---
 drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/rtw8723b.c | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/rtw8723b.c b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/rtw8723b.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/rtw8723b.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/rtw8723b.c
@@ -2361,8 +2361,7 @@
 	/* No firmware hardware feature report exists, so fill it in here. */
 	efuse->hw_cap.nss = 1;
 	efuse->hw_cap.ant_num = 1;
-	efuse->hw_cap.bw = BIT(RTW_CHANNEL_WIDTH_20) |
-			   BIT(RTW_CHANNEL_WIDTH_40);
+	efuse->hw_cap.bw = BIT(RTW_CHANNEL_WIDTH_20);
 
 	return 0;
 }
